WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.249
Idea. A drinks company wants a can that holds a fixed amount of juice with as little metal as possible. To optimise means to find the largest or smallest value of something. Here, the metal is the surface area.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:27.373
The surface area depends on two letters, r and h. To differentiate we need one. So we use the fixed volume to write h using r, and substitute. That is eliminating a variable.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:39.755
Cylinder volume is pi r squared times h. A closed cylinder has two circular ends and a curved side. Unrolled, the side is a rectangle, two pi r by h.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:49.652
These questions say show that S equals a given formula. The answer is printed, so show every line, ending exactly at the printed result.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:01:03.150
Method. Step one: write the formula for the fixed amount, and the formula for the target, using both letters. Step two: rearrange the fixed one to make the unwanted letter the subject.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:13.126
Step three: substitute. Step four: simplify to the printed result. The trap is a missing face: a closed cylinder has two circles.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:31.859
Worked example. January twenty twenty two. A solid cylinder has radius r and height h. Its volume is ninety pi and its total surface area is S. Show that S equals two pi r squared, plus one hundred and eighty pi over r. Three marks.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:41.802
Step one: the volume. Pi r squared times h equals ninety pi. Divide by pi r squared: h is ninety over r squared.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:51.189
Step two: the target. Total surface area is two circles, plus the curved side. Two pi r squared, plus two pi r h.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:02:00.752
Step three: substitute h equals ninety over r squared. Two pi r squared, plus two pi r times ninety over r squared.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:12.785
Step four: simplify. Two pi r times ninety is one hundred and eighty pi r. Over r squared, one r cancels, leaving one hundred and eighty pi over r.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:23.416
A tempting wrong route is to count only one circular end. That gives pi r squared, not the printed answer. Total surface area needs both ends.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:35.434
So S equals two pi r squared plus one hundred and eighty pi over r, as required. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:50.787
Common mistake. A common mistake is an algebra slip at the end: two pi r times ninety over r squared becomes one hundred and eighty pi over r squared. One r cancels, so the answer is over r.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:03:09.209
Your turn. Now you try, from June twenty twenty five. A solid cylinder has total surface area seven hundred pi. Show that the volume V is pi r times three hundred and fifty minus r squared. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:27.352
Answer. The surface area is fixed: two pi r squared plus two pi r h equals seven hundred pi. Divide by two pi: r squared plus r h equals three hundred and fifty. So h is three hundred and fifty minus r squared, over r.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:43.881
The target is the volume: pi r squared times h. Substitute: pi r squared times, three hundred and fifty minus r squared, over r. One r cancels. That is pi r times three hundred and fifty minus r squared.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:55.083
The most common wrong answer uses only one circle in the surface area. Then h comes out differently, and the printed result cannot be reached. Count every face.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:02.152
Remember: fixed formula, rearrange, substitute into the target, simplify to the printed result.
